1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,840
Okay, welcome back. Primary immunodeficiency. This is the next section in Chapter 2 that we want to cover,

2
00:00:07,280 --> 00:00:11,560
and the first disorder that we're going to talk about is DeGeorge syndrome, very high yield.

3
00:00:12,140 --> 00:00:17,760
This is a developmental failure of the third and fourth pharyngeal pouch, so this really goes back to embryology,

4
00:00:17,860 --> 00:00:23,120
and it's a mechanism by which they can test you on embryology, and it's essentially associated,

5
00:00:23,120 --> 00:00:29,980
first important point is that it's associated with a 22Q11 microdeletion, and that's the

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:36,980
highest high yield to be aware of. So failure to form the third and fourth pharyngeal pouch,

7
00:00:37,080 --> 00:00:43,520
which is basically associated with a 22Q11 microdeletion. Now, important to know that these

8
00:00:43,520 --> 00:00:50,060
patients are going to present with classic findings of the lack of the structures that are derived from

9
00:00:50,060 --> 00:00:55,060
the third and fourth pharyngeal pouch. For example, the thymus is derived from that region, and so if

10
00:00:55,060 --> 00:00:59,980
there is a lack of a thymus, there'll be a T cell deficiency. Now, that T cell deficiency occurs

11
00:00:59,980 --> 00:01:05,920
because remember that T lymphocytes or T cells are actually born from the bone marrow but then go to the

12
00:01:05,920 --> 00:01:12,460
thymus to become educated and to eventually become CD4 positive or CD8 positive T cells, and so if there

13
00:01:12,460 --> 00:01:17,980
is no thymus, there'll be a T cell deficiency, and so that's one important finding in these patients.

14
00:01:18,600 --> 00:01:23,980
Of course, if there's a T cell deficiency, the patients will have problems fighting viruses, and they'll have

15
00:01:23,980 --> 00:01:30,580
problems fighting fungal infections as well. Another important finding in these patients will be

16
00:01:30,580 --> 00:01:36,200
hypocalcemia. Why? Because the parathyroids are normally developed in the third and fourth pharyngeal pouch,

17
00:01:36,560 --> 00:01:42,320
so if there's a lack of parathyroids, there'll be a problem with maintaining adequate levels of calcium

18
00:01:42,320 --> 00:01:49,580
within the serum, hence hypocalcemia. And then finally, within that region from the third and fourth pharyngeal pouch,

19
00:01:49,580 --> 00:01:57,000
important things that also develop include the great vessels, areas of the lower face, the heart,

20
00:01:57,260 --> 00:02:03,060
the anterior aspect of the heart, sorry, the superior aspect of the heart, etc. So all of this kind of

21
00:02:03,060 --> 00:02:09,500
makes sense if you understand a little bit of embryology. The next disorder is called SCID. This is

22
00:02:09,500 --> 00:02:15,800
severe combined immunodeficiency, and basically the idea here is that the patients get defective both

23
00:02:15,800 --> 00:02:21,700
cell-mediated, i.e. T cell, and humoral, i.e. B cells, because they produce antibodies, which is

24
00:02:21,700 --> 00:02:28,740
called humoral immunity. So in SCID, you get defective cell-mediated and humoral immunity. Now, what are the

25
00:02:28,740 --> 00:02:34,960
etiologies? There's three major etiologies. Number one, cytokine receptor defects. Now, you know, and we've

26
00:02:34,960 --> 00:02:43,240
already told you to make it very clear, that the CD4-positive T cell, it is a helper T cell, and it helps

27
00:02:43,240 --> 00:02:51,460
the B cells to become mature and to fight infection, and it helps the CD8-positive T cells to also become

28
00:02:51,460 --> 00:02:56,780
activated to fight infection. So cytokines, which are produced by the helper T cells, you can see how

29
00:02:56,780 --> 00:03:02,260
important they are, because it's the cytokines that drive the maturation of the entire chronic

30
00:03:02,260 --> 00:03:07,540
inflammatory state. So if there are defects in cytokine receptors, patients can develop severe

31
00:03:07,540 --> 00:03:13,140
combined immunodeficiency. Very high yield, for example, and I would say probably the most high yield on this

32
00:03:13,140 --> 00:03:20,420
slide, is an adenosine deaminase deficiency. Adenosine deaminase is an enzyme that's necessary

33
00:03:20,420 --> 00:03:26,540
for deamination of adenosine and deoxyadenosine. This goes back to a little bit of biochemistry and

34
00:03:26,540 --> 00:03:32,400
salvage pathways. But basically, what you need to know is that adenosine deaminase deaminates

35
00:03:32,400 --> 00:03:38,300
adenosine and deoxyadenosine, and that the buildup of adenosine and deoxyadenosine within lymphocytes

36
00:03:38,300 --> 00:03:43,740
is actually toxic to lymphocytes. And so patients who have adenosine deaminase deficiency can get

37
00:03:43,740 --> 00:03:49,760
severe combined immunodeficiency, and this becomes very high yield because it goes back

38
00:03:49,760 --> 00:03:54,760
to biochemistry. And of all of these etiologies, for me, this is the highest yield. And the third

39
00:03:54,760 --> 00:04:02,800
mechanism by which we can get a severe combined immunodeficiency would be an MHC class 2 deficiency.

40
00:04:02,800 --> 00:04:08,380
Now, think about this for a second. MHC class 2 is recognized by which type of T cell? It is

41
00:04:08,380 --> 00:04:15,660
recognized by the CD4 positive T cell. And the CD4 positive T cell is essential because it helps CD8

42
00:04:15,660 --> 00:04:21,480
positive T cells and it helps B cells. And so if the CD4 positive T cell cannot get activated because

43
00:04:21,480 --> 00:04:28,640
MHC class 2 is missing, once MHC class 2 is missing, we're going to get defective function of B cells,

44
00:04:28,640 --> 00:04:34,320
defective function of T cells. And so I think that clearly explains why these patients would have a

45
00:04:34,320 --> 00:04:41,160
mixed humoral and cell-mediated deficiency. All right, so these are some of the key etiologies

46
00:04:41,160 --> 00:04:45,580
to be aware of. Now, what are the characteristics? So these patients don't have T cells, so they're

47
00:04:45,580 --> 00:04:50,440
going to get fungal and viral infections. And these patients don't have B cells, so they're going to get

48
00:04:50,440 --> 00:04:56,320
bacterial and protozoal infections. And there's going to be an increased risk for opportunistic

49
00:04:56,320 --> 00:05:00,620
infections. They're sort of like an AIDS patient, but much worse. So they can get opportunistic

50
00:05:00,620 --> 00:05:05,340
infections. And you want to be aware of giving these patients live vaccines because they essentially

51
00:05:05,340 --> 00:05:10,780
have no immune system. How do we treat? These patients go in sterile isolation. They're also

52
00:05:10,780 --> 00:05:15,820
called the bubble baby. And you give them a stem cell transplant. Now, why does a stem cell

53
00:05:15,820 --> 00:05:19,920
transplant work? Because if you put stem cells in their bone marrow that are normal, let's say

54
00:05:19,920 --> 00:05:25,180
you take stem cells from another patient and put them into this patient's bone marrow, those stem

55
00:05:25,180 --> 00:05:30,320
cells, you know, the hematopoietic stem cells, so hematopoietic stem cell here, has the ability

56
00:05:30,320 --> 00:05:37,340
to generate both B and T cells. And so the patient will then generate normal B and T cells and will

57
00:05:37,340 --> 00:05:42,360
then obviously be able to develop the immune response necessary to be able to fight off

58
00:05:42,360 --> 00:05:49,740
infections. So the sort of the temporary measure is isolation, sterile isolation, a quote-unquote

59
00:05:49,740 --> 00:05:56,640
to a bubble baby. And the final treatment is a stem cell transplant. Another immunodeficiency

60
00:05:56,640 --> 00:06:02,140
to be aware of is X-linked A-gamma globulinemia. Let's look at the name. A means without, gamma

61
00:06:02,140 --> 00:06:08,600
globulin refers to immunoglobulin, and where? In the blood. So these patients are lacking immunoglobulin

62
00:06:08,600 --> 00:06:14,740
within the blood. And it's X-linked. So this is due to a lack of immunoglobulin. It's due to a disordered

63
00:06:14,740 --> 00:06:21,260
B cell maturation so that naive B cells cannot become plasma cells. Remember that it's the

64
00:06:21,260 --> 00:06:26,480
plasma cells that actually have the ability to secrete immunoglobulin into the blood. And

65
00:06:26,480 --> 00:06:32,260
so naive B cells cannot mature to plasma cells and hence cannot drop immunoglobulin into the

66
00:06:32,260 --> 00:06:37,000
blood. And so that's X-linked A-gamma globulinemia. And I would say that the highlight here is

67
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:42,740
that the mutation is a tyrosine kinase mutation. In particular, it's called Bruton tyrosine kinase,

68
00:06:42,740 --> 00:06:49,260
and it's also called BTK. So Bruton tyrosine kinase is a tyrosine kinase that is a signal,

69
00:06:49,260 --> 00:06:54,340
the tyrosine kinases are signaling molecules. So it is a signaling molecule that is necessary

70
00:06:54,340 --> 00:07:01,320
for a B cell to become a plasma cell. When it's mutated, and it's usually X-linked, that results

71
00:07:01,320 --> 00:07:07,140
in this particular disease called X-linked A-gamma globulinemia. Now how will these patients present?

72
00:07:07,140 --> 00:07:12,720
Well, they're going to get bacterial infections, enterovirus infections, and giardia. I would

73
00:07:12,720 --> 00:07:17,680
burn this in my brain. When I'm lacking immunoglobulins, I'm going to get three types of

74
00:07:17,680 --> 00:07:22,620
infections. Bacteria, because I don't have the ability to opsonize properly because I

75
00:07:22,620 --> 00:07:30,040
don't produce IgG. Enterovirus and giardia. Bacteria, enterovirus, and giardia. And enterovirus

76
00:07:30,040 --> 00:07:36,160
basically is a virus that affects the mucosal surface of the GI tract. And remember that IgA

77
00:07:36,160 --> 00:07:41,120
protects against mucosal infection of the GI tract. And these patients aren't going to have

78
00:07:41,120 --> 00:07:46,080
IgA, so they'll get enterovirus. And giardia is the same thing. It usually produces a GI

79
00:07:46,080 --> 00:07:51,080
infection. Mucosal IgA would be protective against it. And so these patients are going

80
00:07:51,080 --> 00:07:55,500
to present with these classic findings. Usually, it's after six months of life. Why after six

81
00:07:55,500 --> 00:08:03,000
months of life? Because the patient has mom's antibodies floating around prior to that. The

82
00:08:03,000 --> 00:08:07,460
antibodies last for a period of time from mom. And so they usually don't present until after

83
00:08:07,460 --> 00:08:11,940
six months of life. And you have to be careful in these patients. You don't want to give them

84
00:08:11,940 --> 00:08:17,260
live vaccines because their immune system is down and those live vaccines, whereas a normal

85
00:08:17,260 --> 00:08:22,820
patient would be able to deal with an attenuated live polio vaccine, these patients certainly

86
00:08:22,820 --> 00:08:27,480
would not be able to handle this vaccine. And it would eventually cause complications of

87
00:08:27,480 --> 00:08:32,120
that infection within the patient. Another important disease is called common variable

88
00:08:32,120 --> 00:08:39,100
immunodeficiency. Now, listen to the name. Common variable, i.e., the patient has low immunoglobulin.

89
00:08:40,120 --> 00:08:45,400
And this is due to a B cell defect or a helper T cell defect. Why B cells? Because B cells produce

90
00:08:45,400 --> 00:08:51,640
immunoglobulin. Why helper T cells? Helper T cells produce IL-4 and IL-5 when the appropriate signal

91
00:08:51,640 --> 00:08:58,620
is present. And so both helper T cells and B cells are necessary to properly produce immunoglobulin.

92
00:08:58,620 --> 00:09:03,400
Now, again, these patients have low immunoglobulin. So what are they going to get? Bacteria enterovirus

93
00:09:03,400 --> 00:09:09,320
giardia. That's why I say burn that in your brain. Bacteria enterovirus giardia. In addition,

94
00:09:09,480 --> 00:09:16,720
these patients are often asymptomatic and they usually present late in adulthood. And as they

95
00:09:16,720 --> 00:09:22,020
continue on with their life, there's an increased risk for autoimmune disease and lymphoma. And

96
00:09:22,020 --> 00:09:29,360
that's high yield. An increased risk for both autoimmune disease and lymphoma. Okay, the next

97
00:09:29,360 --> 00:09:35,820
disorder is called IgA deficiency. And the idea here is that the patients have a low serum and

98
00:09:35,820 --> 00:09:41,460
mucosal IgA. This is the most common immunoglobulin deficiency. And guess what kind of infections

99
00:09:41,460 --> 00:09:48,300
they're going to get? Mucosal infections. IgA is on the surface of, is on mucosal surfaces and helps to

100
00:09:48,300 --> 00:09:53,160
protect against mucosal infections, especially viral infections. And so these patients will get

101
00:09:53,160 --> 00:09:59,220
an increased risk of mucosal infections, especially viral. Now, maybe we could stop here for a second.

102
00:09:59,320 --> 00:10:04,980
I can tie in a little bit, do a little bit of circular review. Do you know which GI disease is

103
00:10:04,980 --> 00:10:12,820
associated with IgA deficiency? Otherwise stated, in which GI disease is there a high propensity of

104
00:10:12,820 --> 00:10:19,820
patients who have IgA deficiency? The answer is celiac disease. And that's something to be familiar with

105
00:10:19,820 --> 00:10:26,480
clinically, as well as for examinations. So in celiac disease, one of the associations of celiac disease

106
00:10:26,480 --> 00:10:32,680
is IgA deficiency. And we'll talk about that when we do the GI lectures as well. Okay, hyper-Igm syndrome.

107
00:10:32,680 --> 00:10:37,320
I actually like this one. This shows up a lot on exams. It's a great way to test a little bit of normal

108
00:10:37,320 --> 00:10:43,640
immunology. So what's the idea here? It's characterized by too much IgM. And the key feature

109
00:10:43,640 --> 00:10:50,860
here is that the patients have a mutation in the CD40, something to do with CD40, CD40 ligand or CD40

110
00:10:50,860 --> 00:10:56,700
receptor. So let's go back and review for a minute. Can we remind ourselves that there were two ways by

111
00:10:56,700 --> 00:11:00,800
which B cells get activated? I'm going to wipe this out just to go back and do a little review.

112
00:11:01,200 --> 00:11:05,480
There were two ways, as I talked about in the prior lecture, in which B cells can be activated.

113
00:11:05,480 --> 00:11:11,400
The first way is for a naive B cell, which basically looks like this and expresses IgM on its surface,

114
00:11:11,500 --> 00:11:17,000
correct? And I said that one way for this cell to get activated is that an antigen binds IgM

115
00:11:17,000 --> 00:11:25,380
on the surface, and that that then results in an IgM-secreting plasma cell. And that plasma cell

116
00:11:25,380 --> 00:11:32,900
can then produce IgM. So that's one way by which the B cell, sorry, by which, yeah, by which the B cell

117
00:11:32,900 --> 00:11:38,920
can be activated. Okay? So bear that in the back of your mind. A second way, I'm going to go back and

118
00:11:38,920 --> 00:11:44,220
give you another whiteboard here. A second way by which the B cell can be activated is that the B cell,

119
00:11:44,680 --> 00:11:49,900
so let's pretend that this is the B cell, can actually internalize the antigen and present the

120
00:11:49,900 --> 00:11:59,820
antigen instead on, let's say that this is MHC class II, right? So if the B cell presents the antigen

121
00:11:59,820 --> 00:12:06,900
on MHC class II, the CD4 positive T cell will bind that antigen, and that will result in the first

122
00:12:06,900 --> 00:12:12,180
stimulus. But remember that there's a second stimulus that occurs, and that is a CD40 stimulus,

123
00:12:12,300 --> 00:12:20,680
and that's really what you have to remember. The CD40 will be, is basically expressed by the B cell,

124
00:12:20,980 --> 00:12:28,760
and it binds CD40 receptor on the T cell, and it's the binding of CD40 to its receptor that provides the

125
00:12:28,760 --> 00:12:34,740
second signal, so that the CD4 positive T cell can get activated, CD4 positive T cell, and when the

126
00:12:34,740 --> 00:12:41,380
CD4 positive T cell gets activated, it's going to produce IL-4, remember the two key ones are IL-4

127
00:12:41,380 --> 00:12:48,740
and IL-5, and IL-4 and IL-5 will basically go back and hit the B cell, allowing the B cell to mature

128
00:12:48,740 --> 00:12:56,000
and to undergo class switching, so that eventually that B cell can produce IgA, IgG, and IgE.

129
00:12:56,000 --> 00:13:01,620
Okay, with all of that background, now we go back to this disease, which is called hyper-IgM syndrome.

130
00:13:01,940 --> 00:13:07,160
It's characterized by an elevated IgM. In this particular disease, the first mechanism of

131
00:13:07,160 --> 00:13:12,420
activation of B cell is okay. It's intact. So that what can happen is antigen can bind the B cell,

132
00:13:12,500 --> 00:13:18,240
and the B cell can produce IgM. However, the second way of activating B cell, which was dependent on CD40,

133
00:13:18,240 --> 00:13:26,200
is mutated. Either CD40 is mutated or the CD40 receptor is mutated, but the problem is that that

134
00:13:26,200 --> 00:13:32,560
second signal cannot be delivered to the helper T cell. If the second signal cannot be delivered to

135
00:13:32,560 --> 00:13:40,440
the helper T cell during B cell activation, then those cytokines, i.e. IL-4 and IL-5, necessary for Ig class

136
00:13:40,440 --> 00:13:47,140
switching cannot be produced. So IgA will be low, IgG will be low, and IgE will be low. But IgM will be

137
00:13:47,140 --> 00:13:52,480
high. Why is IgM high? Because the very first mechanism of activating the B cells, where they

138
00:13:52,480 --> 00:13:57,720
overproduced IgM, is intact. All right, so that's the way to remember this. Now what's going to happen?

139
00:13:57,780 --> 00:14:03,600
Because IgA is low, you're going to get mucosal infections. Because IgG is low, you're going to

140
00:14:03,600 --> 00:14:09,580
recurrent pus forming infections, because IgG is an opsonin. So these are some of the key features

141
00:14:09,580 --> 00:14:14,820
and clinical features that would be present in this disorder. Very high yield, okay? Hyper IgM

142
00:14:14,820 --> 00:14:21,400
syndrome. It's very high yield because it allows for examiners to go back and test you on some basic

143
00:14:21,400 --> 00:14:26,580
principles of immunology. Okay, and then, and it really goes downhill from here. So Wiscott-Aldrich

144
00:14:26,580 --> 00:14:31,600
syndrome, basically you just have to sort of memorize that this is characterized by a triad,

145
00:14:31,600 --> 00:14:36,640
and that triad is thrombocytopenia. That's number one. So they get an increased risk of

146
00:14:36,640 --> 00:14:42,360
bleeding. They can get petechia in the skin, for example. Petechia in the skin and mucosal

147
00:14:42,360 --> 00:14:47,760
membrane bleeding. They get eczema, so they'll get a skin rash. And the patients get recurrent

148
00:14:47,760 --> 00:14:52,800
infections, and they can get, they have defective both humoral and cellular immunity, so they

149
00:14:52,800 --> 00:14:58,880
get a variety of infections, all right? So that's the Wiscott-Aldrich syndrome. This is due

150
00:14:58,880 --> 00:15:04,720
a mutation in the Wiscott-Aldrich syndrome protein. No rocket science. Wiscott-Aldrich

151
00:15:04,720 --> 00:15:11,860
syndrome protein, and that is X-linked, okay? Another group, another circumstance in which

152
00:15:11,860 --> 00:15:17,220
we may find immunodeficiency is where there is complement deficiency. There's really two

153
00:15:17,220 --> 00:15:22,680
high yields here. Number one, if a patient is deficient in anything from C5 to C9, so C5,

154
00:15:22,680 --> 00:15:28,520
C6, C7, or C8, or C9, there'll be an increased risk of Neisseria infection, and that is high

155
00:15:28,520 --> 00:15:34,900
yield, okay? That's one. And then two, if the patients have a deficiency in something called

156
00:15:34,900 --> 00:15:45,020
C1 inhibitor, they will develop edema of skin and mucosal surfaces, and that's called hereditary

157
00:15:45,020 --> 00:15:50,520
angioedema. Now what's the idea here? Remember that C1 is an important activator of complement,

158
00:15:50,520 --> 00:15:55,520
and it's an important early protein in the complement pathway, and when it gets activated,

159
00:15:55,640 --> 00:16:02,440
it turns on complement. Now in order to decrease the potential risk of activation of C1, we have

160
00:16:02,440 --> 00:16:07,020
something called C1 inhibitor, which helps to sort of control C1 so that it doesn't get activated

161
00:16:07,020 --> 00:16:12,240
except when necessary. But if the inhibitor is deficient, we're going to get over-activation of C1.

162
00:16:12,680 --> 00:16:17,680
Over-activation of C1 will over-activate complement, and the consequence of over-activation of complement

163
00:16:17,680 --> 00:16:23,520
will be vasodilation, increased vascular permeability, and some of the classic findings

164
00:16:23,520 --> 00:16:29,440
of acute inflammation, particularly the formation of edema. And to these patients, the consequence

165
00:16:29,440 --> 00:16:34,840
is something called hereditary angioedema. The classic finding is edema of the skin and mucosal

166
00:16:34,840 --> 00:16:40,520
membranes, especially periorbital. That's classic. And so I threw in a little picture here. This is a little

167
00:16:40,520 --> 00:16:46,360
picture in which we see that this child's eyes are edematous, or the skin surrounding the eyes

168
00:16:46,360 --> 00:16:54,200
is edematous. And this is a classic image of hereditary angioedema. And that really closes off

169
00:16:54,200 --> 00:16:58,860
this particular section of immunodeficiencies. And so we'll go ahead and stop there, give you a little

170
00:16:58,860 --> 00:17:02,300
chance to catch up, and then we'll continue on with autoimmune disorders.

171
00:17:02,300 --> 00:17:04,300
